            <ArticleTitle>An infected urachal cyst--a rare diagnosis in a child with acute abdominal pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>587-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00431-011-1622-3</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="UNLABELLED">A 2-year-old girl who presented with acute abdominal pain and spiking fever was diagnosed with an infected urachal cyst. Ultrasonography aided the diagnosis and the urachal remnant was removed successfully through a single laparoscopic procedure. Treatment is through removal of the complete structure, to prevent malignant degeneration in adulthood.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Urachal cysts may cause abdominal complaints when infected. Although rare, they should be added to the differential diagnosis of acute abdominal pain in the paediatric patient, as this case illustrates.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
